WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.809
When Carrie Fisher passed away in December
2016, fans were understandably heartbroken

00:00:06.809 --> 00:00:10.920
and we all mourned the loss of our favourite
Star Wars princess and general.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.099
Carrie had completed filming her scenes for
The Last Jedi and now we've had a chance to

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:19.160
see Leia back on screen in an extremely poignant
performance.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.859
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I want to take a look at Carrie

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:27.989
Fisher's role in The Last Jedi and how Lucasfilm
could conclude Leia's story in Episode 9.

00:00:27.989 --> 00:00:32.640
I'll be discussing mild spoilers from Leia's
scenes in the movie, but I'll avoid any major

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.030
plot reveals.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:37.010
I'm also running my Star Wars giveaway on
this video.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.609
To enter, make sure you're subscribed to my
channel and leave me a comment below with

00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:43.149
your thoughts about Carrie Fisher or Leia.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.739
And I'll announce the winner of one of my
recent giveaways later in the video.

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:52.170
Before I get to Leia returning for Episode
9, I want to quickly review Carrie Fisher's

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.879
role in The Last Jedi.

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:58.160
If you've seen the movie, you'll know Leia
has several scenes that really hit you emotionally,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.710
plus there are a couple of poignant moments
with Luke and Rey.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.890
And although these scenes play out nicely,
her final moment is particularly sad when

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.500
you consider that Leia was originally going
to be at centre of the story for Episode 9,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.110
in a similar way to how Han Solo was for Episode
7 and Luke was for Episode 8.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.750
After Carrie died, Lucasfilm has had to go
back and rewrite the story for the final chapter

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.750
in the new trilogy.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:26.130
Still, I'm glad that Carrie and Leia had a
significantly bigger role in The Last Jedi

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.060
over her relatively brief appearance in The
Force Awakens.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:34.220
But Leia's story in the new trilogy still
feels unfinished, so how will Lucasfilm complete

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.250
her arc?

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.780
One possible option is using the hour or so
of deleted and unused footage that Rian Johnson

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.610
shot for The Last Jedi.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.310
According to Carrie Fisher's brother Todd,
he and her daughter Billie Lourd have granted

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.450
LucasFilm permission to use recent footage
of Carrie in Episode 9.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.200
And perhaps for dialogue, they could reach
into the Lucasfilm archives as they did for

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.040
Rogue One or use actors who've voiced Leia
in other Star Wars properties.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.500
Leia's dialogue was already a technical challenge
for Episode 8 as Carrie died before she was

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.890
able to perform Additional Voice Recording,
or ADR, which is normal for any film and involves

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.869
actors re-voicing lines, for example, if the
on-set audio isn't good enough.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:14.860
Rian Johnson has said that post-production
for Leia's scenes was tough and they had to

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.110
do a lot of sound work to resolve issues.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.640
I didn't notice anything especially strange
with Leia's dialogue in the movie, so maybe

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.050
it could be possible, though labour intensive,
with smart sound work and various audio-tricks

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.120
to create some realistic-sounding dialogue
for Leia in Episode 9.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.880
Now as much as some fans might like to see
Leia in the next film, in April 2017, Kathleen

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.482
Kennedy said Carrie won't appear in the final
instalment, though Todd Fisher has said that

00:02:41.482 --> 00:02:45.400
he hopes she reconsiders this, and, let's
face it, Lucasfilm have changed their plans

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.720
during production before, just think of Rogue
One, the Han Solo film, and even Episode 9

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.720
itself.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.990
Also, recently, Mark Hamill has been telling
fans we can expect a fitting and respectful

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.200
end to Leia's story in the next film.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.950
So, Lucasfilm might be tempted to include
a small appearance, depending on fan reaction

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.209
after episode 8.

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:09.450
Another option, which I think many fans wouldn't
like, is a CGI Leia, as happened with Grand

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.470
Moff Tarkin and young Leia in Rogue One.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:17.000
Those CGI characters had a mixed reception
and Lucasfilm were quick to shut down speculation

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.120
after Carrie's death about a CGI Leia appearing
in Episode 9.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.720
Nevertheless, a recast hasn't been ruled out,
however, I think this is most unlikely given

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.569
Carrie Fisher's popularity, unless it's limited
to, say, just a few shots of a stand-in from

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:31.569
behind.

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:35.890
So far, I've talked about ways Leia could
appear in the final movie, however, another

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.630
realistic option is that her story gets closure
in another way.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.910
This would probably mean that when Episode
9 begins, we learn that Leia has perhaps passed

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:48.880
away or retired off screen and so she's no
longer around with the Resistance.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.130
It could be referenced via the opening crawl,
for example Leia is reported as having been

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.740
assassinated by the First Order and perhaps
they could even show General Organa's funeral.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.750
It would certainly be one way to kick off
the movie and pay tribute to Carrie Fisher

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.520
at the same time.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.850
This would likely mean we're looking at a
time jump of at least several months, if not

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:10.790
a few years, between the end of episode 8
and the start of 9.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:15.080
This could also serve other storylines for
the final chapter of the new trilogy and I

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.979
think with some imaginative storytelling,
it would still be possible for Leia to be

00:04:18.979 --> 00:04:21.160
at the heart of the final episode.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.060
For example, the Resistance defeating the
First Order could be linked back to Leia's

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.139
legacy, with protagonists like Rey or Finn
referring back to her at key moments in the

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:30.139
story.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:34.279
I also noticed that Carrie Fisher's daughter
Billie Lourd was given a bigger part as Lieutenant

00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:38.740
Connix in The Last Jedi and I think her character
could be part of an important tribute scene

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.819
in the next movie.

